I'll implement `proverb` to accept arbitrary positional arguments plus a keyword-only `qualifier`. Each consecutive pair produces a verse, and the final line uses the first item, prefixed by the qualifier when one is given (e.g. "And all for the want of a horseshoe nail." with qualifier "horseshoe").

proverb.py
```python
def proverb(*inputs, qualifier=None):
    if not inputs:
        return []

    verses = [
        f"For want of a {first} the {second} was lost."
        for first, second in zip(inputs, inputs[1:])
    ]

    if qualifier:
        last = f"And all for the want of a {qualifier} {inputs[0]}."
    else:
        last = f"And all for the want of a {inputs[0]}."

    verses.append(last)
    return verses
```

I'm implementing a `chess_board` class following the standard Exercism C++ queen-attack interface: a constructor taking white and black positions (throwing `std::domain_error` if they are identical or off the board), accessors `white()` and `black()`, `can_attack()`, and a string conversion via `operator std::string`.

queen_attack.h
```cpp
#if !defined(QUEEN_ATTACK_H)
#define QUEEN_ATTACK_H

#include <string>
#include <utility>

namespace queen_attack {

using position = std::pair<int, int>;

class chess_board {
   public:
    chess_board(position white, position black);

    position white() const;
    position black() const;

    bool can_attack() const;

    operator std::string() const;

   private:
    position white_;
    position black_;
};

}  // namespace queen_attack

#endif // QUEEN_ATTACK_H
```

queen_attack.cpp
```cpp
#include "queen_attack.h"

#include <cstdlib>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace queen_attack {

namespace {
bool valid(const position& p) {
    return p.first >= 0 && p.first < 8 && p.second >= 0 && p.second < 8;
}
}  // namespace

chess_board::chess_board(position white, position black)
    : white_(white), black_(black) {
    if (!valid(white) || !valid(black)) {
        throw std::domain_error("position off the board");
    }
    if (white == black) {
        throw std::domain_error("queens cannot share a position");
    }
}

position chess_board::white() const { return white_; }

position chess_board::black() const { return black_; }

bool chess_board::can_attack() const {
    int dr = std::abs(white_.first - black_.first);
    int dc = std::abs(white_.second - black_.second);
    return dr == 0 || dc == 0 || dr == dc;
}

chess_board::operator std::string() const {
    std::string result;
    for (int r = 0; r < 8; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 8; ++c) {
            if (c > 0) result += ' ';
            position p{r, c};
            if (p == white_)
                result += 'W';
            else if (p == black_)
                result += 'B';
            else
                result += '_';
        }
        result += '\n';
    }
    return result;
}

}  // namespace queen_attack
```

I implemented `queen_attack::chess_board` with the interface I recall from the Exercism C++ tests. I haven't run it against them, so two details are guesses:

- **Constructor:** it takes two `std::pair<int, int>` positions (white, then black) and throws `std::domain_error` if they are the same square or off the 8x8 board.
- **Accessors and attack check:** `white()` and `black()` return the positions. `can_attack()` is true when the queens share a row, a column or a diagonal.
- **String output:** `operator std::string` renders the board as 8 lines, each with space-separated cells and a trailing newline, using `_` for an empty square, `W` for white and `B` for black. I assumed the pair is `(row, column)`, and that format is from memory.

