I implemented all five functions in `promises.js`. I couldn't run the exercise's tests, so two behaviours are guesses from memory of the exercise.

- **`promisify`**: wraps a callback-style function and returns a function that gives back a `Promise`. It appends an error-first callback to the arguments, then rejects on an error or resolves with the data.
- **`all`**: resolves with an array of results in input order, and rejects as soon as any promise rejects. An empty array resolves with `[]`.
- **`allSettled`**: resolves once every promise has settled, with `{ status: 'fulfilled', value }` or `{ status: 'rejected', reason }` objects in input order.
- **`race`**: settles with the first promise to settle.
- **`any`**: resolves with the first fulfilled value, and rejects only once every promise has rejected.

Guesses to check against the tests:
- If no argument is passed, `all`, `allSettled` and `any` resolve with `undefined`.
- `any` rejects with a plain array of the errors in input order, not an `AggregateError`.

I wrap each item in `Promise.resolve(...)` so plain values work too; none of the built-in `Promise.all`, `allSettled`, `race` or `any` methods are used.

promises.js
```javascript
//
// Implementation of the 'Promises' exercise.
//

export const promisify = (fn) => {
  return (...args) =>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      fn(...args, (error, data) => {
        if (error) {
          reject(error);
        } else {
          resolve(data);
        }
      });
    });
};

export const all = (promises) => {
  if (promises === undefined) {
    return Promise.resolve(undefined);
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = Array.from(promises);
    const results = new Array(items.length);
    let remaining = items.length;

    if (remaining === 0) {
      resolve(results);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then((value) => {
        results[index] = value;
        remaining -= 1;
        if (remaining === 0) {
          resolve(results);
        }
      }, reject);
    });
  });
};

export const allSettled = (promises) => {
  if (promises === undefined) {
    return Promise.resolve(undefined);
  }

  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const items = Array.from(promises);
    const results = new Array(items.length);
    let remaining = items.length;

    if (remaining === 0) {
      resolve(results);
      return;
    }

    const settle = (index, result) => {
      results[index] = result;
      remaining -= 1;
      if (remaining === 0) {
        resolve(results);
      }
    };

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(
        (value) => settle(index, { status: 'fulfilled', value }),
        (reason) => settle(index, { status: 'rejected', reason }),
      );
    });
  });
};

export const race = (promises) => {
  if (promises === undefined) {
    return Promise.resolve(undefined);
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    Array.from(promises).forEach((item) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, reject);
    });
  });
};

export const any = (promises) => {
  if (promises === undefined) {
    return Promise.resolve(undefined);
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = Array.from(promises);
    const errors = new Array(items.length);
    let remaining = items.length;

    if (remaining === 0) {
      reject(errors);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, (error) => {
        errors[index] = error;
        remaining -= 1;
        if (remaining === 0) {
          reject(errors);
        }
      });
    });
  });
};
```

I implemented all five functions in `promises.js`. I haven't run it or any tests. The four built-in re-implementations avoid calling `Promise.all`, `Promise.race` and the like. They use only the `Promise` constructor and `.then`.

- **`promisify`**: returns a function that calls the original with the given arguments plus a callback. The callback rejects on an error and resolves with the data otherwise.
- **`all`**: resolves with results in input order once every promise has resolved, and rejects on the first rejection. An empty or missing input resolves with `[]`.
- **`allSettled`**: resolves with `{status, value}` or `{status, reason}` objects once every promise has settled. An empty or missing input resolves with `[]`.
- **`race`**: settles with whichever promise settles first.
- **`any`**: resolves with the first fulfilled value. If all reject, it rejects with an `AggregateError` holding the errors in input order, or with a plain array if `AggregateError` is unavailable. An empty or missing input rejects immediately in the same way.

Each input is wrapped in `Promise.resolve`, so plain values work as well as promises.

promises.js
```javascript
export const promisify = (fn) => {
  return (...args) =>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      fn(...args, (error, data) => {
        if (error) {
          reject(error);
