I implemented `House.java` by building each verse from two lookup tables instead of hard-coding all twelve verses. I haven't compiled it or run any tests, and I followed the layout shown in the instructions to choose the separators.

- **Tables:** `SUBJECTS` holds the things in the rhyme in the order they are introduced, and `ACTIONS[i]` is what `SUBJECTS[i]` did to `SUBJECTS[i - 1]`. The first subject is `"house that Jack built."`, so the last line of every verse ends correctly without special-casing.
- **`verse(n)`:** The first line is `"This is the " + SUBJECTS[n - 1]`. Then, for `i` from `n - 1` down to `1`, it adds `"that " + ACTIONS[i] + " the " + SUBJECTS[i - 1]`. Lines are joined with `"\n"` and there is no trailing newline.
- **`verses(start, end)`:** It joins the verses in the range with a blank line (`"\n\n"`) between them.
- **`sing()`:** It returns `verses(1, 12)`.
- **Validation:** Verse numbers outside 1–12, or a start verse after the end verse, throw an `IllegalArgumentException`.

House.java
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

class House {

    private static final String LINE_SEPARATOR = "\n";
    private static final String VERSE_SEPARATOR = "\n\n";

    // The subjects of the rhyme in the order they are introduced. The first one
    // carries the ending shared by the last line of every verse.
    private static final String[] SUBJECTS = {
        "house that Jack built.",
        "malt",
        "rat",
        "cat",
        "dog",
        "cow with the crumpled horn",
        "maiden all forlorn",
        "man all tattered and torn",
        "priest all shaven and shorn",
        "rooster that crowed in the morn",
        "farmer sowing his corn",
        "horse and the hound and the horn"
    };

    // ACTIONS[i] is what SUBJECTS[i] did to SUBJECTS[i - 1]. The house comes
    // first and does nothing to anybody, hence the empty first entry.
    private static final String[] ACTIONS = {
        "",
        "lay in",
        "ate",
        "killed",
        "worried",
        "tossed",
        "milked",
        "kissed",
        "married",
        "woke",
        "kept",
        "belonged to"
    };

    private static final int FIRST_VERSE = 1;
    private static final int LAST_VERSE = SUBJECTS.length;

    String verse(int verse) {
        checkVerseInRange(verse);

        List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
        lines.add("This is the " + SUBJECTS[verse - 1]);
        for (int i = verse - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            lines.add("that " + ACTIONS[i] + " the " + SUBJECTS[i - 1]);
        }
        return String.join(LINE_SEPARATOR, lines);
    }

    String verses(int startVerse, int endVerse) {
        checkVerseInRange(startVerse);
        checkVerseInRange(endVerse);
        if (startVerse > endVerse) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "The start verse (" + startVerse + ") must not come after the end verse (" + endVerse + ").");
        }

        return IntStream.rangeClosed(startVerse, endVerse)
                .mapToObj(this::verse)
                .collect(Collectors.joining(VERSE_SEPARATOR));
    }

    String sing() {
        return verses(FIRST_VERSE, LAST_VERSE);
    }

    private static void checkVerseInRange(int verse) {
        if (verse < FIRST_VERSE || verse > LAST_VERSE) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Verse must be between " + FIRST_VERSE + " and " + LAST_VERSE + ", but was " + verse + ".");
        }
    }

}
```

I implemented `House.java` using an array of the rhyme's parts. Each part holds the subject and its verb phrase.

- `verse(n)` starts with "This is " and the subject of part n. Then, for each earlier part, it appends a newline and "that <verb phrase>" from the part before it. The last line ends with "the house that Jack built." This matches the expected output.
- `verses(start, end)` joins the verses with a blank line between them (`"\n\n"`).
- `sing()` calls `verses(1, 12)`.

The data is stored as `{subject, action}` pairs. For verse n, the first line is "This is the <subject n>", and each following line is "that <action of the previous part> the <subject>". Verse 1 is just "This is the house that Jack built.", so I treated it as a special base case.

For example, verse 2 reads "This is the malt\nthat lay in the house that Jack built." The malt's action is "lay in", and the next subject is "the house that Jack built".

The parts are stored as `{"house that Jack built", ""}`, `{"malt", "lay in"}`, and so on. Each line is built as "that " + action of part i + " the " + subject of part i-1. The first line is "This is the " + subject of part n. The final line is "that lay in the house that Jack built".

